WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.433
OpsPilot turns an alert into a structured
investigation, with people still in control of
change.

00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:16.516
This Checkout API case is a seeded local replay. The
evidence is synthetic, not production telemetry.

00:00:16.896 --> 00:00:26.004
The investigator reads only incident-scoped
evidence: logs, metrics, traces, and deployment
changes.

00:00:26.384 --> 00:00:35.678
All thirty-two database pool connections are
occupied. Checkout waits one point eight four
seconds before acquisition fails.

00:00:36.058 --> 00:00:45.560
Database connection pool exhaustion is the supported
cause. Evidence is linked, while competing
explanations remain visible.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:59.318
OpsPilot proposes one typed, allowlisted action:
restore the seeded pool baseline to sixty-four
connections. The investigator cannot write, approve,
or execute it.

00:00:59.698 --> 00:01:08.096
A different approver reviews the exact parameters.
Then the operator runs it once, only in the
simulator.

00:01:08.476 --> 00:01:16.964
Server-owned checks decide if the incident resolves.
The approval and verification remain in the incident
record.

00:01:17.344 --> 00:01:23.908
Evidence first. Action under human control.
OpsPilot.
